Let H be a hypothesis class indexed by a parameter θ ranging over a measurable space Θ. A prior on H is a (σ-finite) measure π on Θ; π is called proper if π(Θ) < ∞ (and may then be normalized to a probability measure), and improper otherwise. A prior assignment rule is said to satisfy the symmetry principle when, for any finite Θ, it assigns equal mass to every θ ∈ Θ (the uniform distribution). For "compact" smooth parametric families, Jeffreys' prior π_J(θ) ∝ √det I(θ), where I(θ) is the Fisher information matrix, provides a canonical reparametrization-invariant extension of this symmetry principle, but on non-compact parameter spaces such as Θ = ℕ or Θ = ℝ it generally yields an improper prior (e.g. Lebesgue measure on ℝ, or counting measure on ℕ), which is often unacceptable for inductive inference because it cannot be normalized and may fail to yield well-defined posterior predictive probabilities.

Problem. Formulate a general principle for assigning a proper prior π on an arbitrary (possibly countably infinite or non-compact continuous) parameter space Θ that:
(i) reduces to the uniform distribution when Θ is finite;
(ii) is invariant under smooth reparametrizations of Θ (i.e. transforms as a measure under bijective sufficiently regular changes of coordinates);
(iii) is proper, i.e. π(Θ) < ∞, on classes such as Θ = ℕ and Θ = ℝ;
(iv) extends or agrees with Jeffreys' prior in the regular compact parametric case.

Either exhibit such a rule together with a characterization theorem, or prove that no rule simultaneously satisfying (i)–(iv) can exist.

